### Verification Lab: Measuring Stepped-Down Logic Levels

Before connecting a delicate 3.3V radio module, you should verify that your breadboard voltage divider correctly steps down the 5.0V signal. Connect the output of your $1\text{k}\Omega / 2\text{k}\Omega$ divider to Arduino analog input **A0**:

```cpp
// Monster Lab - Voltage Divider Logic Shifter Tester
const int TEST_SIGNAL_PIN = 11; // Simulates 5V UART TX pin
const int VOLTAGE_PROBE_PIN = A0; // Probes divider center tap

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(TEST_SIGNAL_PIN, OUTPUT);

  Serial.println("========================================");
  Serial.println("VOLTAGE DIVIDER VERIFICATION ACTIVE");
  Serial.println("========================================");
}

void loop() {
  // Drive output HIGH (+5.0V)
  digitalWrite(TEST_SIGNAL_PIN, HIGH);
  delay(10); // Allow voltage to stabilize

  // Read analog voltage at center tap
  int rawAdc = analogRead(VOLTAGE_PROBE_PIN);
  float measuredVolts = (rawAdc * 5.0) / 1023.0;

  Serial.print("Input: 5.00V | Raw ADC: ");
  Serial.print(rawAdc);
  Serial.print(" | Divided Output: ");
  Serial.print(measuredVolts, 2);
  Serial.print(" V");

  if (measuredVolts >= 3.1 && measuredVolts <= 3.5) {
    Serial.println(" --> [SAFE FOR 3.3V RADIO RX]");
  } else {
    Serial.println(" --> [DANGER: CHECK RESISTOR VALUES!]");
  }

  delay(1500);
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
1. `digitalWrite(TEST_SIGNAL_PIN, HIGH)` simulates the continuous 5.0V HIGH idle state of an Arduino UART TX line.
2. `(rawAdc * 5.0) / 1023.0` calculates the exact voltage measured at the junction between R1 and R2.
3. The conditional logic verifies that the output sits safely in the 3.1V–3.5V safe operating window.
